// apps/rtq's three queries over the set of triangles -- the nearest triangle
// a ray hits, whether it hits any, the triangle closest to a point -- as
// Embree and FCPW answer them. rtq.bonsai exports batches of each; this
// file is the queries alone, so that another program can ask one of them
// (apps/wosx asks `closest` at every step of its walk) without rtq's
// exports. Declared over elements.bonsai and a metrics file compiled
// before it, as rtq.bonsai's header says.

// Embree: TravRay -- the ray as the traversal reads it, its segment clamped
// to [0, inf) at entry (`tnear = max(tnear, 0)`, `tfar = max(tfar, 0)`,
// node_intersector1.h). A box's entry distance is `max(.., tnear)`, so with
// the clamp it is never negative, and the sort's key can be ordered as its
// bits without the sign flip that a negative float would need: Embree's
// `distance_i` is `asInt(tNear)` for the same reason; and the traversal's
// running best can be folded into `tfar` on the bits (SSA/Simplify.cpp)
// because the best is a non-negative number. The compiler carries the facts
// from here through the calls to the key and the node test
// (SSA/ConstantIntervals.h, SSA/SortRecursion.cpp), and for that the clamp
// has to leave a non-negative number: Embree's `max(tnear, 0)` is std::max,
// which hands a NaN or a negative zero through unchanged, and Embree rests
// instead on asserting the ray valid (`ray.valid()`: `tnear >= 0`). The
// clamp here is the same for every such ray and says so to the compiler:
// what is above zero stays, everything else -- negative, negative zero, not
// a number -- is zero.
func clamped(r : Ray) -> Ray = Ray{r.o, select(r.tnear > 0.0, r.tnear, 0.0), r.d,
                                  select(r.tfar > 0.0, r.tfar, 0.0)};

// Embree: rtcIntersect1; FCPW: Scene::intersect -- the nearest triangle the
// ray hits, if any, with the reference's record of the hit. The map
// annotates every candidate with `hit_record(r, t)`, the record of the same
// test `intersects` names and `distmin` keys, so the hit the argmin answers
// carries its record: the three ask one question of a candidate, the leaf
// evaluates them together and CSE keeps one test, and the argmin holds the
// record by value beside its reference to the triangle. That is where
// Embree makes its record too (MoellerTrumboreHitM::finalize in the leaf,
// for each hit nearer than the best), and where FCPW makes its point,
// normal and uv (for every lane of every packet) -- not a second test on
// the winner after the traversal, which cost 6-18% on primary rays. The
// same shape as apps/pbrt's trace.
func trace(r0 : Ray) -> option[(Triangle, Hit)] {
    r = clamped(r0);
    return argmin(|t : Triangle, h : Hit| distmin(r, t),
                  filter(|t : Triangle, h : Hit| intersects(r, t),
                         map(|t : Triangle| (t, hit_record(r, t)), triangles)));
}

// Embree: rtcOccluded1; FCPW: Scene::intersect with checkForOcclusion --
// whether the ray hits anything at all.
func occluded(r0 : Ray) -> bool {
    r = clamped(r0);
    return any(|t : Triangle| intersects(r, t), triangles);
}

// Embree: rtcPointQuery with the closest-point tutorial's callback; FCPW:
// Scene::findClosestPoint -- the triangle closest to the point. Both walk
// the tree ordered by the children's distance and shrink a radius to each
// closer triangle found; the argmin here is that search, its running best
// the radius, and the lowering prunes by it as both do: a child is entered
// only where its box is nearer than the best, and the best is lowered to
// the farthest a child's box can be, which FCPW does at every node
// (enqueueNodes) and Embree's callback form cannot. Every triangle the set
// holds is a candidate: which stored slots are triangles is the layout's
// to say, not this query's. The map annotates each candidate with the
// reference's record (the closest point, its distance), the same
// computation `distmin` keys, so one evaluation serves both, as for trace.
func closest(q : Point) -> option[(Triangle, ClosestHit)] {
    return argmin(|t : Triangle, c : ClosestHit| distmin(q, t),
                  map(|t : Triangle| (t, closest_record(q, t)), triangles));
}
